 "source_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cy013eyKiBs",
 "title": "How to Handle a Shorter Presentation Time",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "When presentation time gets cut short unexpectedly, people often rush or panic, which loses the main message. The creator claims the real problem is building presentations from the slides up, and recommends working backwards by starting with core questions and a one-minute version.",
 "key_points": [
  "When a meeting time is cut short, people usually either talk twice as fast or panic and delete slides, which causes the main message to get lost.",
  "The root problem is building the presentation from the slides up.",
  "Before opening PowerPoint, answer four questions known as 'know, think, feel, do': What do I need them to know, what do I need them to think, how do I want them to feel, and what do I want them to do?",
  "Write the one-minute version of your presentation first by saying it out loud to ensure you have a solid core message.",
  "Build out the presentation from the one-minute version by adding examples, evidence, stories, and details.",
  "When your time is cut down, you know exactly what extra details to drop while keeping your core message intact."
 ],
 "content": "The creator discusses what happens when a presentation slot is suddenly cut short, such as when a meeting runs over and a CEO has a hard stop. Most people respond by talking twice as fast or panicking and deleting slides two minutes before starting, which causes the main message to get lost. The creator states that the real problem starts much earlier because people build their presentations from the slides up. Instead, you should work backwards. Before opening PowerPoint, the creator recommends answering four questions: 'What do I need them to know', 'What do I need them to think', 'How do I want them to feel', and 'What do I want them to do'. Next, you write the one-minute version first by saying it out loud. If it works in one minute, you have a core message. From there, you build it out by adding examples, evidence, stories, and details that people need to trust you and think you are credible. When your ten minutes become five, you know what to drop because it is the extra detail that goes, while the core message stays.",
 "status": "done",
